Morning light spilled across the freshwater lake in a broad wash of gold and copper, fracturing against the gentle current into a thousand shifting coins that stretched toward the island in the distance.  The Academy of Mythrandis stood against the dawn — its central towers catching the first true light of the day, pale stone turning the color of warm honey, banners snapping lazy and bright from parapets too high to read.

The bridge was a quarter-mile of white stone and iron railing, wide enough for a carriage and team, though no carriage had ever crossed it.  That was the tradition.  You walked.  You entered as yourself, stripped of title and retinue and the comfort of a family name shouted ahead of you.

Brycendar Symboline walked its center like a king reviewing a parade ground.

Four figures trailed in his wake — close enough to hear, far enough back to frame him as the point of the formation.  Serwyn kept pace at his right shoulder, sandy hair freshly combed, uniform pressed to knife-edges, his soft features arranged into eager attention.  Graeven held the left, grey eyes sweeping the bridge ahead and the water below with the practiced disinterest of someone cataloging everything.  Behind them both, Lethane slouched with his hands in his pockets, dark hair uncombed, his blazer already hanging open despite the morning chill.  Rashten brought up the rear, broad shoulders rolling with each heavy step, copper-red hair catching the light like a struck match.

*Six months.*

"I had the most extraordinary dream last night," Brycendar said.  His voice carried across the stone without effort — measured, unhurried, the cadence of someone who had never once worried about being talked over.  The lake wind stirred his blonde hair across his forehead.  "Seven women.  Each more beautiful than the last.  And every single one of them —" He smiled, not at his companions, but at the towers ahead.  "— madly, irreversibly in love with me."

*Six months of learning the weight of this body.  The reach of these arms.  The depth of a mana reserve that felt bottomless because he'd made it that way — had approved the numbers on a design document between a board meeting and a lunch he couldn't even remember.*

Serwyn laughed immediately, bright and admiring.  "Seven, Your Highness?  That's ambitious even for you."

"It isn't ambition, Serwyn.  It's trajectory."  Brycendar adjusted the sword at his hip — Velmontis, the Symboline family blade — without looking down.  The gesture was automatic.  Proprietary.  "Ambition implies uncertainty.  This felt more like remembering something that hasn't happened yet."

*He hadn't played the game.  Hadn't needed to.  He'd signed the contracts, approved the lore bible, glanced at the character art for three seconds during a quarterly review and moved on to earnings.  The broad strokes were all that mattered: this prince ends up with seven women.  That was the product description.  And now he was the product.*

*No.  Better than that.  He was the version that knew the ending.*

"Blended mana, a crown, and a face like that," Rashten rumbled from the back, grinning wide enough to show his back teeth.  "Seven might be low, Your Highness."

Brycendar's smile widened — a fraction, perfectly controlled.  "That's the spirit, Rashten.  Though let's keep our feet on the ground."  He lifted his right hand.  A flame bloomed in his palm, golden-white, steady as a lantern against the lake wind.  He closed his fist and it died.  Water beaded across his knuckles next, condensing from nothing like dew forming in fast motion.  He shook it free and the breeze that followed wasn't the lake's — it rolled from his open fingers, a clean gust that ruffled Serwyn's immaculate hair and sent the boy scrambling to smooth it back.

Three elements.  Casual as breathing.

*Three disciplines.  He'd written the system so the protagonist would carry all three — martial swordsmanship, fire, water, wind.  Sea-level mana reserves.  The kind of talent that didn't occur in nature because it wasn't supposed to.  It was supposed to sell copies.*

"Do you know what the faculty calls a first-year Blended with reserves like mine, Graeven?"  He didn't wait for the answer.  "Paper-worthy.  The kind of talent they publish about.  The kind they study."

"Deservedly so, Your Highness."  Graeven's grey eyes held steady, his tone carrying the exact weight of sincerity required.  Not a grain more.  "There may not be another student on this island who can fight every range."

"No," Brycendar agreed, and there was nothing false in his certainty.  "There won't be."

*Because there wasn't supposed to be.  The protagonist stands alone at the top.  That was the pitch deck.  The investor slide.  Bryce had built his company on the principle that a clear market leader eliminated confusion, and he'd built this prince the same way — dominant in every category, unchallenged by design.*

*Bryce had inherited a corner office.  Brycendar had inherited an empire.  Same man.  Better hand dealt.*

"My father speaks with the Headmaster directly," Brycendar continued, his boots striking the bridge stones in an even rhythm.  He did not look down.  "Helvanthor's reach extends even here, gentlemen.  Our military, our alliances, our political networks — there isn't a noble family on this island whose fortunes haven't been shaped by a Symboline decision in the last century.  Remember that.  Carry it in how you walk, how you speak, how you present yourselves.  You don't simply attend this academy.  You represent the crown."

Lethane yawned.

Brycendar's gaze cut to him.  Held a beat.  Two.  Three.

"Lethane."  Warm.  Patient.  The patience of someone who has never needed to repeat himself but has chosen to, this once, as a kindness.  "Straighten your blazer.  You represent Helvanthor before you represent yourself.  I shouldn't have to remind you."

*He never had to remind people at the company, either.  They fell in line because the alternative was expensive and everyone understood that without it being said.  Structural power didn't require force — just presence.  Just the understanding that things had an order, and that he was at the top of it.  Always had been.  Both lives.*

They were past the bridge's midpoint now.  The island's western shore was taking shape ahead — stone quays, guard posts flanking worked-iron gates, the formal entrance to the academy grounds.  Beyond it, pathways wound between manicured gardens toward buildings of pale marble and warm timber, and students filled them — hundreds of colored blazers streaming inward like tributaries feeding a river, first-years obvious by the way they couldn't stop looking up at the towers.

Brycendar looked ahead.  Only ahead.

"There."  His chin lifted — a small, precise motion.  He didn't point.  Pointing was for people who needed to draw attention to themselves to be noticed.  "The woman with the white hair.  Do you see her, Serwyn?"

She was difficult to miss.  Pale as carved marble, white hair pulled into a high ponytail that fell to her thighs, moving through the crowd in stiletto heels with a stride that struck the ground like a verdict.  Students parted around her without being asked, the way water breaks around the prow of a ship and doesn't know why.  A large sapphire glinted at her throat, cold blue against skin that had never seen a kind sun.

Aivriel.  The Northern Queen.  Last of her bloodline, crowned at seven, more governance experience than most of the faculty who would be pretending to teach her.

"She came here to forge alliances," Brycendar said.  His voice warmed with something that sounded very much like admiration.  "She'll find one."

*Asset.  High-value.  Combat-capable, politically essential, the north's entire future wrapped in one woman.  She didn't know it yet — didn't know that her presence at this academy, at this exact moment, was a piece already placed on a board she couldn't see.  A timing issue.  Nothing more.*

"And there —"  His gaze tracked left, finding crimson in the crowd like a drop of blood on white linen.  Short, sharp-featured, pale skin made vivid by deliberate makeup, stacked jewelry catching the morning light in a cascade of tiny fires.  She moved through the other students the way a card dealer moves through a deck — touching everything, belonging to nothing.  Evangelise Torricelli.  The Hundred Isles' most promising export, wrapped in silk and walking the grounds like the entire island was a market floor and she was deciding what to buy.

"The Torricelli heir.  Crimson hair, crimson eyes."  A note of amusement entered his voice, perfectly placed, like a musician touching a grace note.  "She thinks she's the one doing the reading."

Serwyn followed his gaze, rising on his toes.  "She's stunning, Your Highness."

"She's positioned."  The word landed with the clean precision of a file being sorted into its correct place.  "Two of seven, Serwyn.  And the morning is young."

*Two.  Identified.  Cataloged.  Roles assigned.  Five more somewhere on this island — arriving now, or already here, or waiting just around a corner he hadn't turned yet.  The game hadn't provided him a timeline.  Just a certainty: they would be his.  All seven.  Because that was how the story had been written, and he was the one who'd signed off on it.*

They passed through the iron gates.

The grounds opened before them — a sweep of cultivated green and stone pathways, garden beds heavy with late-summer blooms, the central towers rising above everything like a promise etched in stone.  Students clustered in knots of nervous conversation.  A boy in an ill-fitting blazer was trying not to stare at his own sleeves.  A girl with pointed ears — elven, her silver hair cut bluntly at her jaw — walked alone and unhurried, as if she had been doing this for decades.  She probably had.

Brycendar drew a breath.  Clean air.  Stone and water and flowers and the faint residual warmth of old magic baked into every surface.  His academy.  His stage.

*The starting zone.  The opening act.  Where the protagonist meets the cast, establishes himself, and sets the trajectory for everything that follows.  He didn't need to know the details.  Details filled themselves in around a man who knew where the story was going.*

"Your Highness."

The voice came from his right — smooth, polished, and utterly without interest.

She stood just inside the entrance to the first building.  A maid.  Black dress, white apron, the skirt cut precisely above the knee.  High neckline covering her throat.  Long sleeves ending in black lace gloves.  White stockings.  Stiletto heels that could have left marks in the stone.  Her dark hair was pinned beneath a headpiece that framed her face without softening it, and her makeup was flawless in the way that a blade's edge is flawless — not decorative, but maintained.

She held a leather folio against her chest the way a soldier holds a shield.  Not defensively.  As a matter of course.

"First-year students are to proceed to the eastern hall for orientation," she said.  Her tone was flat and polished.  Not rude.  Not warm.  The exact register of someone directing foot traffic who would be directing foot traffic long after he had passed.  "Dormitory assignments will follow tea.  Left corridor, please."

Brycendar stopped.

His entourage stopped behind him — Serwyn immediately, Graeven a half-step later, Rashten nearly walking into Lethane, who had not been paying attention.

"I appreciate the guidance," Brycendar said.  He offered her the smile he'd been wearing all morning — warm, gracious, carrying a faint downward tilt that read as genuine condescension only if you knew what to look for.  "However, I believe you'll find the crown prince of Helvanthor has arrangements that precede the general orientation.  My father corresponded with the Headmaster directly regarding my arrival.  If you could direct me to his office, I'll —"

"The eastern hall."  She did not blink.  Did not shift.  Did not append *Your Highness* or *begging your pardon* or any of the small verbal genuflections that had cushioned every interaction he'd experienced in this body or the last one.  "All first-year students.  Left corridor."

She turned to the next cluster of students filing through the door behind him.  "First-years, eastern hall.  Left corridor, please."

Dismissed.

Not argued with.  Not refused.  Dismissed — the way one redirects a river by simply being the stone it flows around.  Smoothly, totally, and without the faintest acknowledgment that the river had opinions about the matter.

Serwyn's mouth hung slightly open.  Graeven's eyes had narrowed to thin, calculating lines.  Rashten's massive hand had curled into a fist at his side, the knuckles whitening.

Brycendar stood very still.

His smile had not changed.  His posture had not shifted.  His hands remained exactly where they were — one resting on Velmontis' pommel, the other at his side, fingers relaxed.  Not a single muscle in his face had moved.

From the outside, he looked like a crown prince exercising admirable restraint.

*Servants don't argue back.  There's no cruelty in putting down a dog that forgets who feeds it.*

He adjusted his collar.  One precise motion.

"Eastern hall, then," he said, and walked left.
